Description: the canvas of a stereo image per eye, through a stereo GtkGLArea
 A stereo image is an image with the top-level layers "Left eye" and "Right
 eye" (how the stereo pictures plug-in opens MPO, JPS and PNS files), or a
 picture holding both views side by side or top and bottom that the person
 put into one of the plug-in's editing modes (the parasites
 stereo-pictures/edit-mode and stereo-pictures/layout).  While View > Enable
 3D is on (it is by default), such an image's canvas is drawn twice, once for
 each eye, each from that eye's own content at the canvas's own size: the
 shell's render code renders each eye into a render cache of its own from the
 eye's sources (the eye's layer, or the eye's view of the packed picture in
 both halves, so the canvas keeps the image's size and coordinates), and
 scrolling moves those caches as it moves the shell's own.  The two pictures
 go to the compositor through a stereo GtkGLArea (the edition's GTK 3, which
 hands them over side by side, left first, declared once as full side by side
 stereo content, on a surface of its own below a hole in the window).  The
 area lies under the canvas items (GimpCanvas's underlay, drawn by the shell
 between the backdrop and the items), so guides, the selection, paths and the
 tools' outlines stay over the picture at the screen, the same in both eyes.
 Each eye's picture carries what lies under the canvas in 2D (the backgrounds
 of the widgets around it).  When the area cannot show the canvas per eye,
 the canvas draws the left eye itself, with its items over it, and the person
 is told why in the status bar (the reason in English on stderr); turning
 Enable 3D off and on tries again.  On X11 the renderer is first read on a
 window of its own: with Mesa's zink GTK 3 draws an X11 window that holds an
 OpenGL area black, so there the image window gets no area.  View >
 Display Settings... opens the display settings (kcmshell6 kcm_kscreen),
 where each screen's 3D mode is chosen.  On X11 the image window gets an
 alpha channel when the screen is composited, so that the stereo child window
 can lie below it.  A 2D image, and every image with Enable 3D off, is drawn
 as before.  The feature is built only with a GtkGLArea that has
 gtk_gl_area_set_stereo() (meson: HAVE_GTK_GL_AREA_STEREO, OpenGL through
 epoxy).
Author: Daniel Campos Ramos <Capitain_Jack@yahoo.com>
Origin: other, Sparky Stereo OS (queue row 137, the stereo GIMP's milestone 1)
Forwarded: no
Last-Update: 2026-11-26
